In-Reply-To: <icudNGC1olE.W5FBiWTWstgJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <icudNGC1olE.W5FBiWTWstgJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/icudNGC1olE/m/RwCKnJfuHg8J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Kasper,

You can almost get the width of a panel:

library(ggplot2)
qplot(mpg, wt, data = mtcars, facets = vs ~ am)

grid.ls <http://grid.ls>()
# Pick a panel grob
# (These aren't named very useful in this version but I'll work on that)

panel <- grid.get("panel.gTree.654")
downViewport("background")
downViewport("panels")
convertWidth(grobWidth(panel), "cm")

But unfortunately it gives a width of 0 cm.  I don't understand why,
so I'll talk to Paul Murrell about it.

What I'm planning for a future release is the complement of your wish
- the ability to set the width of individual components (e.g. panels
or legends).  That should make it easier to align multiple plots with
varying numbers of panels and legends.  It's not currently possible
because of the way that the viewports are constructed - I first make a
viewport layout for the panels & axes, and then embed that in another
viewport for the title, legend and axis labels.

Hadley

